NAME
    qemu-image.sh - prepare a disposable GPT QEMU disk image for m-boot

SYNOPSIS
    tests/qemu-image.sh --stage DIR --scenario loader|recovery --output DIR
                        [--bios-modules-dir DIR]

DESCRIPTION
    On a Linux test host running as root, creates a fresh 768 MiB raw image
    using qemu-img and sgdisk; attaches only that file through losetup.
    The image contains a FAT32 ESP and an exFAT data partition. BIOS/GPT
    additionally contains a 10 MiB BIOS Boot Partition (type EF02). The script formats
    and mounts partitions inside the image, then installs the chosen test
    scenario. It never receives or opens a physical target disk pathname.
    Output file: DIR/m-boot-TARGET-SCENARIO.raw; never replaced silently.

SCENARIOS
    loader       The EFI fixture calls install.sh --apply on the image's
                 mounted ESP and data partition; the ESP contains
                 /m/boot/loader/init. BIOS copies the loader to ESP and embeds its
                 GRUB core into the image's BIOS Boot Partition.
    recovery     Only the EFI image (UEFI) or BIOS GRUB core (BIOS) is
                 installed; no external loader is present. The embedded
                 recovery is the expected boot result.

REQUIREMENTS
    Linux root with working loop/partition/mount support; qemu-img, sgdisk,
    losetup, mkfs.vfat, mkfs.exfat, mount/umount, findmnt and filesystem
    support for vfat and exfat. BIOS also needs grub-bios-setup and a
    matching --bios-modules-dir with boot.img. The stage must come from
    build.sh with matching GRUB modules. This command may create temporary
    loop devices and only writes sectors inside its new raw image file.

EXAMPLE
    sudo tests/qemu-image.sh --stage ./dist/x86_64-efi --scenario loader \
      --output ./qemu-out

LIMITATIONS
    This prepares a virtual disk, not a physical removable drive. If
    a mount or loop detach fails, the script reports the leftover temporary
    path so an operator can investigate. A successful image creation does
    not establish that the firmware has booted the image.

